{
  "image_name": "b03eb1bacd67bfca093395289f0dd245.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two ways to detect chiral small molecules in a mixture by first converting them into more easily measurable forms. In the upper scheme, a mixture of small molecules is treated with chiral derivatizing reagents, producing derivatized chiral molecules while other molecules remain unchanged. These products are then analyzed by NMR. The cartoon spectrum shows two separated signals, labeled D and L, indicating that derivatization creates a measurable chemical-shift difference between enantiomers, so their identities or relative amounts can be distinguished.\n\nThe lower scheme shows an alternative strategy using probes that conjugate specifically to chiral molecules. After probe addition, the target molecules become fluorescent or otherwise detectable conjugates. Detection is then carried out either by a colorimetric assay or by fluorescent imaging. The colorimetric panel suggests a positive sample gives a colored spot compared with a blank or negative control, while the fluorescence panel shows many bright magenta spots corresponding to labeled molecules.\n\nOverall, the image conveys a general workflow: start from complex small-molecule mixtures, selectively modify chiral components, and then read them out using either NMR-based signal separation or optical detection.",
